The standard test launcher now prepares real project inputs before starting tests. Folder selection keeps the existing gate rules and private-input exclusions. Independent review and the final aggregate run remain required before acceptance.

Use repeated `--folder <repository-relative folder>` arguments after `run <gate>` to select a union of folders. Both slash styles work. Missing, unknown, repeated or escaping folder names fail; a folder with no eligible files for that gate also fails. With no folder argument, the selected files remain exactly those of the existing gate. Vitest arguments remain separate and both frameworks keep their own reports.

Each invocation writes `selected-inventory.json` beneath its fresh checkout-local `.runtime/test-gates/run-*` directory. It records every selected file, its source hash, framework and gate, plus excluded files and reasons within the requested folders. Node evidence defaults to that owned directory rather than the shared dependency junction.

Every `full` run, including a named-folder full run, prepares fresh public Miniflare metadata, executes the canonical `setup-oracle-mod-public.mjs` with a fresh owned `--public-dir`, then the existing `build-engine.mjs --browser-hosts`. Other gates do the same when their selected product/browser folders require these inputs. The pinned source resolver selects existing gem source; no Ruby interpreter, software install/download or hosted Bunny call is involved. Setup reads the real pinned public assets and verifies each source/readback byte hash. It never reuses or overwrites the shared oracle public directory.

The runtime metadata step makes one credential-free request to `https://workers.cloudflare.com/cf.json`, rejects redirects and errors, and writes the exact fresh response to the owned root's `node_modules/.mf/cf.json`. It records bytes, SHA-256 and an independently measured fetch duration; a previous cache is retained only as diagnostic evidence. The root dependency directory and cache must be ordinary owned directories, not junctions or redirected paths; rejection precedes any fetch or cache write. Runtime cache selectors are set only for this run's children and restored afterward. A failed fetch never launches tests or substitutes old metadata. This authorized public runtime-data request is separate from software installation and from hosted application testing.

Before building, the launcher retains and removes any previous browser-build receipt. The actual build must produce a new receipt whose source commit, source difference, optional source snapshot and browser-host configuration match the source observed before and after the build. A zero exit without a new receipt, or a plausible receipt from another source, fails. Raw setup/build stdout and stderr and both source-bound manifests are retained under `project-preflight/`. A failed prerequisite starts neither framework.

After successful preparation, Node and Vitest still overlap. Both must settle before config retirement and provider cleanup. Cleanup validates the entire fresh provider before removing anything, unlinks only the four recorded directory links, and removes only its own empty directories and manifest. Windows junctions use guarded `cmd /c rmdir` without recursive flags and with hidden windows; Linux uses unlink. Shared gem targets are never traversed for deletion. The retained manifest remains in the preflight receipt directory.

All durations use independent monotonic measurements. The preflight is labeled as a container that includes setup/build; the two framework phases share an overlap group. Node records event-stream time and separately measured event-report appends and final result writing. `gate-result.json` records the verdict; `gate-timings.json` retains phase durations, overlap labels and the three slowest non-container phases. Its report-writing measurement covers the primary result file, avoiding a fictitious self-timing value for the timing metadata itself. No parallel duration is obtained by subtracting totals.

The frozen ab1 control is retained as `tmp/launcher-ab1-negative-control.json`: the real old CLI body, executed with inert imports, started a framework and forwarded the folder flag without invoking the required preparation hook. It is a control observation, not native application evidence. Regressions cover real pinned asset setup, safe link cleanup, rejected stale/foreign build receipts, failed-input refusal, folder admission, unchanged lifecycle behavior, real isolated release/index attacks and actual Node report timings.
